The private checkout `/tmp/thegn-THE622-counterfactual-20260915` is at parent `62289bb730b1b1a188761ef4b0a3c4b8723f914d`. Its sole worktree delta is the one-line deletion shown in `/tmp/thegn-THE622-counterfactual-20260915.patch`: the call to `thegn_core::connectivity::report_success()` inside `GhCircuit::record_success`. The patch SHA-256 is `65f8e3d70af720a58f205e6c88b3e92aa405066661db7d2787b92e572bf629f1`; the mutant `native.rs` hash is `dce13599ccf296b4f9bd1d8bc4a0152b163042ace3cd5dc1665fe4198e70da69`, matching the source manifest. The source diff is exactly one deletion and passes `git diff --check`.

The direct global helper and test matrix are unchanged. `native_global_connectivity_tests.rs` retains its `connectivity::report_success()` seed, private marker, exact child selector, eleven-case receipt, and `OwnedChild` custody. The mutant has no `report_success` call in `native.rs`, while the fixture seed still calls the global helper once. Protected hashes for connectivity, both native test modules, owned child custody, static CLI support, Cargo.toml, and Cargo.lock all match the manifest. The misleading success comment remains as documented so the mutation remains exactly one removed statement.

The intended semantic failure is deterministic by source. `seed(true)` first establishes Online/zero through the unchanged global helper, then installs the threshold-one policy and reports one failure, asserting Offline/one. The first reachable `errors-only` GraphQL case is classified as a typed GraphQL error with `reached_server = true`; the mutant still resets the local circuit, but no longer publishes global success. The child therefore reaches the assertion `reachable errors-only must publish global success`, observing Offline instead of Online. The parent should reap that nonzero exact child and fail its `SDK fixture failed` assertion with bounded output. A compile failure, stale fixed artifact, marker refusal, timeout, custody failure, or seed failure would not be the intended counterfactual result.

The strict-cleanup limit is explicit. On this semantic failure the parent cannot reach the helper's success-only receipt or `TempDir::close`; ordinary `TempDir` drop remains best-effort cleanup. The counterfactual demonstrates detection of the omitted global-success publication only. It does not establish later cases, fallback counts, transport controls, the complete receipt, or strict normal cleanup. Root must build the mutant from this checkout and run only the ordinary parent selector; the ignored helper must remain child-only. No landing approval follows from this source review.
